ve an outbreak  good point the next question i have here  is uh  more on the viral loader that looking at  the sub genomic regions  in star core v2 correlate with viral  load  well indirectly the more viral load you  also seem to have more soft genomic  rnas so it's not  i think the the message here is more  that they're there the sub genomic rnas  are there which  may be slightly a surprise but they seem  to be there  even late in infection and from at least  for our samples  when we can detect the virus or the  virus rna  excuse me we can also detect the sub  genomic rna so  there is indirectly accolation the more  virus  at least in our samples you also have  more of the sub genomics  but even when you have fairly low level  of virus  you can still detect them at a low level  these subgenomic rnas  probably the last question i have here  is um  you spent a bit of time on the lead  sequence that was  on the last slide  what do you think are the impact of this  elite uh sequence  uh in terms of uh phytogenesis or or  even viral load uh  because these things are very  interesting finding  yes well so the leader sequence all the  coronaviruses have  have a liter sequence around the 70  nucleotides long and it can vary a  little bit maybe from 50 to 100  nucleotides and it's not only the corona  viruses also other viruses have the same  and i guess they need to have it in  order to make these subgenomic  rnas and they evolved kind of into  having a very  very nice way of making their sub  genomic rnas  and if they couldn't so if they didn't  make those they wouldn't make the  proteins from all from the whole three  prime  end of the virus so it means that it  will not make any spike protein which  causes the receptor binding protein and  also the nuclear protein which is a very  important protein for the coronaviruses  so  yes they have the leader and it sits  there and and i guess in the genomic  rna it may look as if it's not really  doing anything but it is actually very  important because it has to  sit there in the front of each of these  sub genomic rnas and it's basically  a way that the virus at least indirectly  makes its messenger rnas  sub genomic rnas and also the whole  virus rna  are basically looking like messenger  rnas so it  may kind of partly fool the cell  to think it's a it's a normal cellular  messenger rna  because well i don't have time to get  into that but it's  it's not just the leader but it's the  way it makes these subgenomic rnas and  it's also  kind of the way that it gets proteins to  sit on it so it basically  a when it's replicating and making them  it's hiding it from the cell  and when it gets into the cytoplasm in  order to make proteins  it's more or less looks like a normal  cell or mason journey  so the the cell in a sense is getting  tricked into not  responding with a severe immune response  thank you dr alex anderson um  i think that's all the time we have for  now however  thank you uh and as a final reminder to  all our audience  of any questions that were submitted and  not answered today  uh we will uh get back to all of you  by email and i would like to thank dr dr  alexander for his time  for sharing his voice on his insight on  the sarco v2 and the epidemiology of  that  so thank you for all the questions  and all the speakers for spending their  time with us we will now  take a 15 minute break before we start  the next session  check out various application tracks  that may be of your interest  at our booth please go and have coffee  this is the booth  learn more about exciting promotion that  are available to you  where you can ask questions to our  thermal officials  representative at each of the segment  booth please also participate  the scavenger hunting game look for  icons in the environment  to win prices there will be a pop-up  survey  appearing in a minute please help us to  complete the survey  and thank you and look forward to speak  to you again  15 minutes later t